          %title
          %h2#overview_of_painlev_transcendents Overview of Painlevé transcendents
          %p
            In the study of ordinary differential equations one of the important things is the behaviour of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monodromies"} monodromies
            and closely related singularities of solutions. In the linear case, the poles possibly appear just at the poles of coefficients of the solutions. In the nonlinear case the solutions can appear elsewhere and generally propagate with change of initial conditions. Very important is if the singularities do not move or monodromies don’t change with change of parameters. An
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+equation"} ordinary differential equation
            (ODE) satisfies the
            %strong Painlevé property
            if all solutions are single valued around every movable singularity (this is more effective way to express that they can be made single-valued functions by uniformization). A class of such “good” nonlinear equations has been defined by
            %strong Paul Painlevé
            (
            %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Painlev%C3%A9"}> wikipedia
            ), who discovered at the end of 19th century a truly remarkable fact that all
            %em second order
            ODEs of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo ″
                  %mo =
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo ;
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi u
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  u'' = F (z; u, u'),
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is rational in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi u
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u,u'
            and analytic in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            and which satisfy the Painlevé property have solutions which can be expressed in terms of well known functions like elementary and hypergeometric functions and only 6 new kinds of
            %em transcendental functions
            called
            = succeed "." do
              %em Painlevé I-VI
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is at most quadratic in
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo ,
                    %mi u
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u,u'
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integrable+systems"} integrable systems
          %p There are also some noncommutative versions which are still purely understood.
          %p
            Painlevé VI is 2nd order nonlinear ODE governing the isomonodromic deformations of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fuchsian+differential+equation"} Fuchsian differential equation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mi z
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi A
                          %mn 1
                      %mi z
                    %mo +
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi A
                          %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %mi z
                        %mo −
                        %mi t
                    %mo +
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi A
                          %mn 3
                      %mrow
                        %mi z
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{d f}{d z} = \left(\frac{A_1}{z} + \frac{A_2}{z-t}+ \frac{A_3}{z-1}\right),
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi sl
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_i\in sl_2(\mathbf{C})
            and parameter
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            varies through
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi P
                      %mn 1
                    %mo \
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{P}^1\backslash\{0,1,\infty\}
